<ul class="org-ul">
<li><b>Community:</b>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>It's really hard to learn Emacs all by yourself. It's obscure, arcane, and overwhelming.
<ul class="org-ul">
<li><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20080726054506/http://blogs.msdn.com/steverowe/archive/2004/11/17/code-editor-learning-curves.aspx">Steve Rowe's Blog : Code Editor Learning Curves</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/emacs/comments/1uf0d2k/the_learning_cliff_and_the_view_from_the_top/">the learning cliff and the View from the Top</a></li>
</ul></li>
<li>Fortunately, we have a strong culture of sharing, starting with the GNU General Public License and continuing through things like literate programming. We have mailing lists, blog posts, videos, meetups, and conferences. One of the best ways to learn Emacs is to learn out loud. Another way is to look over someone's shoulder. It doesn't take a lot of time or money to do the kinds of things that I enjoy doing to connect the Emacs community, and I'd love to see other tech communities experiment with things like that too.
<ul class="org-ul">
<li><a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2025/05/working-on-the-plumbing-in-a-small-web-community/">Working on the plumbing in a small web community</a></li>
</ul></li>
</ul></li>
<li><b>Adaptability:</b> You can customize Emacs to really fit the way you want to work. The more people do this, the easier it gets to adapt Emacs to even more workflows.</li>
<li><b>Incremental improvements:</b> Even small changes add up, and code turns out to be surprisingly reusable. It often pays off beyond the recommendations of <a href="https://xkcd.com/1205/">xkcd: Is It Worth the Time?</a>.</li>
<li><b>Imagination and play:</b> Tweaking Emacs can be lots of fun. Fun is important.</li>
</ul>

