<ul class="org-ul">
<li>M-x doctor:
<ul class="org-ul">
<li><p>
From "Unix Power Tools":
</p>
<blockquote>
<p>
If you have time to waste (and I mean <i>really</i> waste), Emacs has things to keep you occupied. &hellip; If you think you might be in need of a psychotherapist, save your money and try ESC-x doctor RETURN.
</p>
</blockquote>
<p>
I was not actually looking for a psychotherapist.
I think I was seventeen or eighteen, and I was trying to go through all the interesting computer science books in my university's library in the Philippines. I thought it was funny that someone put in that kind of Easter egg. The more I dug around in the source code, the more I was amazed by the things that people had added to Emacs. Anyway, it's interesting how the fun, inconsequential stuff turned out to be the thing that caught my attention.
</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>Planner Mode: <a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2008/01/how-i-got-hooked/">How I got hooked</a>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>I loved seeing how people customized Emacs in so many different ways to fit the way that their particular brain worked, and getting it to fit my brain too.</li>
<li>One time, when I was the maintainer of Planner Mode, I got an email from someone who said something like: "Thank you for maintaining this. By the way, one of your recent changes lost my data and I didn't have any backups, but that's okay, just please fix it, here are some ideas." That kindness mattered a lot to me.</li>
</ul></li>
<li>Emacspeak:
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>In my third or fourth year of university, my laptop screen started glitching. Sometimes it got really low in brightness and contrast, and you could only read it if you tilted it at an extreme angle and looked very carefully. But the Emacspeak folks had figured out how to make it perfectly usable just with speech synthesis, because they needed that as blind or visually impaired users. This turned out to be pretty handy. I not only used that for a fair chunk of my regular writing and coding, but also for my experiments with wearable computing, and for the <a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2003/02/emacspeak-and-irc-linux/">occasional alert</a> when I was paying attention to something else. Speech synthesis was a lot more discreet than the <a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/wp-content/uploads/2013/05/sacha-014.jpg">head-mounted display</a> that I experimented with in 2003.</li>
</ul></li>
